  "worked_solution": "For a continuous random variable $X$ and a function $r(x),$ the rule of the lazy statistician states that \n\n$$\n\\text{E}[r(X)]={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }r(x)f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\nr(x)=\\frac{1}{x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[\\frac{1}{X}] & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }r(x)f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{x}\\cdot 4x{e}^{-2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-2[{e}^{-2x}]_{0}^{\\infty } \\\\  & =-2(0-1) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
